WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.260
Wizards and Warriors, you are Kuros, the Night Warrior, hero of the Books of Excalibur.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:13.840
Your journey begins deep within the woods of Elrond, where even the wind obeys the supreme wizard, Malkil.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:36.080
Nostalgia And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.120
I'm Mike.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.620
I'm Joe. And I'm Sean.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.800
And today we are talking about...

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.380
Wizards and Warriors.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.960
Nice. Great job.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.320
Sean really wanted to go all in, but I...

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.520
I don't think he expected it.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.000
I'd always be ready for that.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.980
You guys clearly did band at some point in your life, because when I conducted that, you guys

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:54.880
were whiffin'. That's true.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:55.960
Yeah, I played alto saxophone.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.120
It's not a big deal. It was trumpet, you know.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.260
You know what's funny?

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.740
You both look like those instruments.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.320
You know what? I take offense.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.520
I look like a trumpet. What about you?

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:04.760
No, no. I play trombone.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.440
Oh, you look like a trombone.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.860
Yeah, yeah. Well, I think what I meant to say was you look like you would have played those instruments.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.800
I get it with that.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.640
I still take offense. I think the only other instrument I would have had for Joe was drummer.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.240
Hmm. You could look like you played drums.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.540
Yeah, trumpet or drummer. Yeah.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.420
Trumpets, you know, it's like...

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.900
That's a very, like, anybody almost.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:23.940
Yeah. It's a trumpet player.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.300
I'm just like a nobody.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.480
Yeah, I got it.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.400
So that's why I added on drummer as like a nice thing, you know.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.220
And Sean could have also been like a cymbals guy.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.180
Cymbals. Just cymbals.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.560
Triangle. I think Sean's like a triangle guy.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.140
Ding. Guys, Wizards and Warriors.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.460
Did you know it was made by a little company we like to call Rare?

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.060
Yes. Yeah.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:50.080
Rare, you know, makers of Banjo-Kazooie, GoldenEye, Banjo-Kazooie Nuts and Bolts.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.480
Perfect Dark Zero Hour.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.060
I don't even know. List them all.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:01:59.340
It sounds like one.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.980
Battletoads. Mmm.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.940
Yes. Killer Instinct.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.380
Nice. Okay. It conquers Bad Fur Day.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.240
Sure. And the other one.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.860
That's true. It conquers Worser Fur Day.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:18.020
Worser. Honestly, that sounds like a good game.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.580
It doesn't. So Rare makes games, but we already know we like them because the only other game we've

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.020
played from them is a little game called Slalom.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.220
Don't even mention this.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.100
Slalom. Why not mention Slalom?

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.900
Slalom. Slalom is currently an essential game.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.520
It is. It's quite controversial.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.060
It is controversial.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:42.300
It was a good game. To make it even more interesting, Wizards and Warriors is the very last game in 1987.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:49.360
So next week we'll be doing our best of 87 episode where we kind of like do a whole review

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.680
of the year, how we felt, what we're looking forward to, and then name our top five games

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:56.580
of 1987.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.720
We do this for 86 and 85 as well.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.340
You can go back and check those episodes out.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.220
They're very comprehensive.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:07.020
So get hype. And also, you know, feel free to send in your thoughts as well.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.800
Yeah. You know, we always like hearing about what people think of their top five.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.300
Yeah. Also, just one thing to throw out there is that we might restructure the essential games

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.420
list. We've been talking about it a couple times.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.940
We might rig the electoral college.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.040
Yeah. So if you're concerned about the elections, don't talk to us.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.780
We can't change them.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.700
But if you're concerned about the essential games list, you can write in about that for sure.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.640
For sure. But by the time you've heard this episode to write in, we will have already recorded the

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:36.840
next episode. You don't know that. Yeah.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.360
I mean, he does know that as a matter of fact.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.220
Oh, well. But they don't know that.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:45.360
They don't know that. I just don't want them to think we're not listening to them when they write in their stuff.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.840
It's a running gag that this show flows.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.160
It ebbs and woes between time.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.460
Yeah. We recorded these back in the 80s.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.800
Yeah. This is actually our first time playing them, but we still picked them all up on release

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:00.780
day. Right. So Wizards and Warriors developed by Rare.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.400
It's their second game and it comes to America before it goes to Japan.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:06.900
Huh. So that's kind of rare.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.120
I think that's how it is.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:09.880
You didn't even know.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.360
No, I did it like a rare.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:12.820
That's pretty rare. Oh.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.500
Thanks, guys. I appreciate my...

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.640
I got like a fan base here.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.140
Yeah, that was... Well, this is because we were in band.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.160
We were in... Yeah.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.480
It was like you yeeted.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.000
That's really cool.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.580
Cool. And another thing about this game, and the only other timbit I really have about Rare,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.460
is that they used graph paper for this game and then would type in all the data.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:33.680
You know who else did that?

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.200
Nintendo. Yeah.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.780
They did that for Super Mario Brothers and for Legends of Zelda.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.180
Kid Icarus, right? Kid Icarus, yep.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:45.200
So, apparently there were better ways to do it by 1987, but Rare, you know, is a small

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.200
company at this point.

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:47.920
The two brothers are running it.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.400
They only afford paper, no computers.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:50.780
Yeah, I'm just thinking like, yeah.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:52.680
Like, that's probably what happened.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:53.940
They sent in the game as a book.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.300
They just had probably one computer and they were like, nobody can do that.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:58.900
Nobody can touch that while we're working on the graph paper.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.160
It was like a choose your own adventure.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.480
It was like, if you want to go left, turn to page 30.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.700
If you want to jump, turn to page 50.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.920
You joke, but there will be a section later that will never sound more relevant than that

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.920
soundbite. Wow. Feel free to reuse it.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.840
Yeah. Acclaim is the publisher on this one.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.600
They're just hopping on everybody's boat lately.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.820
They seem to be publishing all sorts of stuff.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.260
Acclaim. So, that's all I have on the development side.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.300
Can I get some first impressions on Wizards and Warriors?

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:31.880
I wouldn't say that I felt the same about this game as I do about Deadly Towers, but this

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.260
felt like a Deadly Towers to me.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:45.180
Not in the gameplay, not in the setting, but it just had this vibe to it that we'll get

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.320
into later. Great.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:52.660
Joe? I was excited to see that it seemed like it was trying to be an adventure that had a

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:54.820
lot of different environments and you feel like you're going...

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.980
I know I've mentioned before that I'm a sucker for that kind of thing where it feels like

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:02.800
I'm really going through all these landscapes and getting to the castle and making my way

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.880
to the castle. And it did seem like that that was what they're going for.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.060
So, I was pumped. Yeah.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.040
Great. And I mean, how about that opening music?

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.420
How about it? I thought it was great.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.400
That was my first impression.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.700
I was like, oh... Are you talking about menu music or...?

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:17.880
Yeah, yeah, yeah. When you first...

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.240
I'm not sure I remember it. Oh, it's really...

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.040
Trust me. People love this song.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:25.420
Grammy. I heard about it, but when I booted up the game, I was like, wow, that actually is very

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:28.200
impressive. And it's done by none other than...

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:35.360
Wow, it's done by none other than David Weiss, who has done a lot of music in all video games,

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:39.200
not just rare stuff, but is mostly known for his Donkey Kong Country stuff that people

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.620
still rave about. So, anyway, my first impression beyond the music thing is also...

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:50.340
I didn't get a Deadly Towers feel for this, but I also knew how well regarded...

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.860
I mean, this is a trilogy of games, Wizards and Warriors, just on the NES, so it is a big

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.600
series and a lot of people love it.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:58.900
I didn't fall in love with this game on first impressions.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.640
Okay. It...

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:03.180
The first thing I do in a game is attack.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.600
Yeah. You know? I just automatically go on the offensive.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.400
In any video game I have, I have to figure out how to kill everything else.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:11.200
Exactly. That's what video games are for.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.180
And I hated attacking in this game.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:15.800
So... We'll get into that, yeah.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.460
So, that was my first impression was, wow, I'm really not loving this.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:24.680
So, Wizards and Warriors comes with what I would consider not a manual, but a booklet.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:29.100
Yeah. 23 pages, a full story, and then some.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:31.680
Basically a whole...

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.140
Beseechiary? How do you say that?

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.300
Bestiary? Bestiary.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.400
A whole... Like, you get to see everything in the game pretty much.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:38.960
There's really nothing they're hiding from you.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:44.300
You can see every kind of power-up you'll get, every item, in immense detail too.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:47.760
What every single thing is worth in terms of points.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.800
Mm-hmm. Even, like, nice little tips and secrets and stuff like that.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.120
But I believe we have some things that we want to talk about in specifics.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.840
Yeah. So, Sean, what do you have specifics to talk about?

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:09.860
So, I love it when a manual gets into the world, when it could be mistaken for a document from that world.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:16.360
Okay. I like to think of, I mean, the first thing that pops into my head is Twisted Metal Black.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.760
The manual for Twisted Metal Black was like a bunch of medical records from a psych ward.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:22.820
That was cool.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:32.800
Okay. But it also had a bunch of, like, clear information and all the other stuff was sort of adjacent.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:36.140
Okay. It was stuff that you could ignore if you were just looking for the controls or something.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:43.380
Whereas this is just, whoever wrote this manual was writing the book first and just assumed

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:50.360
that, like, as long as he put in a couple references to, like, how you played the game, that it was

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:56.960
enough. But I think this guy has wanted to be a novelist, but he got stuck making these manuals and was

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:58.220
really angry about it.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.640
So, he made us read this 70-page opera.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:08.820
Right. Yeah. I mean, as much as some of it is absurd, I definitely feel like, as a kid, this would

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.880
have been, like, the manual I'm reading in the car on the way home.

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:12.240
I'm like, there's so much in there.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:13.460
There's story in there.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:14.780
I would have been such a sucker for it.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:20.560
This is much needed, actually, in terms of, like, including story in games on the NES.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:25.040
There's just something to be said about the people who read these things on the car ride

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:31.140
home. Yeah, that's true. And this essentially is filled with spoilers, you know, for a game that is essentially, like,

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.900
50 minutes long if you're good at it.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.240
Yeah. You know? Like, it would take a first-time player a lot longer than that.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.460
Yeah. But you can play through it in 50 minutes in total.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:43.440
And you're basically, like, reading what's happening in, like, I mean, it talks about

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.180
the castle, which is the very last level in the game.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.540
But it is on the map when you see that you're going to a castle.

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:54.400
Right. I don't know. I think it's just, like, the way that things are talked about is with a little too much

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:55.200
detail for me. Yeah.

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.240
Because it even continues on, too, to talk about, like, you know, the many lives of Kuros

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.080
and the warrior's white magic.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:06.760
And then there's also this call to action at the end of, like, the time is upon thee.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:08.880
She's waiting and time is short.

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:10.400
And it's like, there's no timer in this game.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.240
Like, you could do whatever you want.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:17.200
I just think that I would be more appreciated if it was written better.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.020
Because I just need to read this segment.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:27.620
Yeah, please. The kingdom of Elrond is riddled with obstacles, both alive, unalive, and even undead.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:29.480
Yeah. What does that mean?

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.500
I don't know what unalive and undead, what's the difference between the two?

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:33.400
What is an unalive thing?

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:34.720
Unalive is just dead things.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:36.020
Yeah, regular old dead things.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:37.260
Alive, dead, and undead.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.200
That makes so much more sense.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:39.320
Okay, no, I think I get it, actually.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:43.700
The unalive things are, like, things that were never alive to begin.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.540
Yeah, like a table. But if you're going to referee.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:52.180
There are rocks. Okay, this table that we're currently sitting around is not unalive because it was never alive.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:53.140
Well, maybe, what is this?

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.500
It was never alive. It's like anti-alive.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.120
It's just not alive.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:58.280
It's non-alive.

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:01.500
I guess we're getting into linguistics now.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.320
I don't know. No, I think you're right.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:04.840
It's definitely a weird sentence.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:06.840
We're just busting your tongue.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.940
And my eyes immediately glanced to that section.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.540
I had no idea what you were about to say, but I was just scrolling through it.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:16.470
For some reason, there's something about unalive being on, like, two separate breaks.

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:20.030
You know, that, like, just all of a sudden you catch around and you're like, no, it can't be.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:26.130
If we're going to get into, like, little tiny things, let me, I mean, not that that's a tiny, but one that I noticed, I just thought was funny.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:29.210
It's just, now I'm just being, like, a bully to the person who wrote this.

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:38.930
Yeah. But, uh, he does say, um, inside the castle are a series of mazes, each more complicated than the next, meaning that they get easier as you go.

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:40.110
Each more complicated.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:42.670
Each more complicated than the next.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:45.690
Yeah. Okay. So the hardest one is what you're going to start with.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.730
It's obviously just like a...

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.230
I've never noticed that. And typo or whatever, but...

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:58.490
This is, this is, if you're being a bully, I'm just being a straight up jerk here, but there's something very funny to me about when, when it does finally break that wall and just starts showing you the controller.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:00.230
And, like, goes into all these things.

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:04.150
It's like, to move, right or left, press the controller, right or left.

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:05.730
To crouch, press the controller down.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.710
To jump, for a diagonal jump, to levitate.

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:10.310
It's like, wait, what, what, what?

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:15.730
You had me with walking right and left, crouching, jumping, to levitate.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:17.350
It's like everything you could ever do.

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:28.610
There's, there's something else in there too that's, let me scroll down to it here, but there's something else in the control section that's like, oh, to restart the game, press start again.

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:29.770
And I was like, what is it talking about?

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:34.250
It just means when you pause the game to start again, like not to restart the game.

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:35.350
So that was a little bit.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:36.590
That's a treasure trove, the whole thing.

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:47.090
But speaking of kind of going into the technical side of things, I did find it funny, off of the like, intense, like, epic intro, in my head, I'm reading that in like an epic voice.

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.390
And the very next thing is preparing for your quest.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:50.710
I'm still reading this epic voice.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.810
Make sure the power switch is off.

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:57.730
Insert Wizards and Mario cartridge as described in your Nintendo Entertainment System.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:12:59.790
Manual, I'm sorry.

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:02.510
Yeah. Number three, turn the power switch on.

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:06.070
To start, push the start button on player one's controller.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:10.830
Have we ever had a game that told us how to put the game into the NES?

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:11.890
I think they all do.

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:15.550
Do they? I don't think this, I don't think, I don't think, I know that there are certain things like,

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:18.250
don't leave it on for a certain amount of time and stuff like that.

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:22.630
But like, this is the kind of thing that is like, we need to put this in the instructions

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:29.790
because in the event of an apocalypse, when civilization rebuilds, they'll need these kind of instructions

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:30.730
to understand.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:32.610
And they call it preparing for your quest.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:37.490
Like it's like a canonical part of the story is putting the cartridge in the very meta of them.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:43.090
I'm actually surprised that before preparing for your quest, there wasn't like a little section on how to read.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:48.290
You know, just explaining like words should be read from left to right.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:49.870
How to buy the game.

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:55.090
I'm sure that was included at some Funkoland or something like that.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:58.690
Anyway, that's enough of the manual, right guys?

00:13:58.810 --> 00:13:59.950
Does anybody else have anything more?

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:01.250
Nothing else. All right, perfect.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.610
So now we're going to talk about the objective.

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:06.730
Okay. And I will personally walk you through this.

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:12.050
All right. As we mentioned in my, when I was reading the back of the box, you're the hero Kuros.

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:17.110
The princess has been captured by the evil wizard Malkyl in the castle Iron Spire.

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:20.470
And you have to go through the forest of Elrond to get to the castle.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.410
But Mike, how do you do that?

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:28.990
Well, it's simple. You got to explore the levels, obtain the three keys and have enough gems to get to the end of each level.

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:35.330
So is this the first or a very early example of a collectathon?

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:38.690
Oh, I've never really thought about that.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:45.230
Okay. Are collectathons, like what's an example of one now?

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:50.570
Sure. Well, Super Mario Odyssey is like an idea of a collectathon because you have to keep getting

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:52.550
all the moons and stuff like that to progress.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:15:00.290
The idea here is that you need a certain amount of gems to unlock things within the level.

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:08.050
And then you need the three keys to get to, to unlock the, to basically get to the damsel in distress.

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:10.690
And then that's how you get to the end of the level.

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:13.050
Or essentially you need the three keys to get to the boss fight.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:15.030
Right. I guess this is the first example.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:19.770
So I just think that's really neat because Rare is known for those collectathons.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.070
They did Banjo-Kazooie.

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:23.390
They did, um, Diddy Kong Racing.

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:25.770
We went through the rest of them in the beginning.

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:26.890
Uh, in the beginning. Yeah.

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:31.610
They're known for that. But then Super Mario 64 popularized, uh, collectathons and stuff.

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:36.250
So I wouldn't say this is like, you know, there's no, there's no necessarily like tally

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:41.430
at the end of the game that says like, you've acquired this many gems, but it's still in many

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:44.090
ways, an idea of just explore the level.

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:48.410
You don't have to get every single thing in the level, which I appreciate.

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:49.650
Most of them though.

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:50.850
You have to get most of them.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:56.450
Yeah. But the idea of just like, here's the level, go and find the things that you need and then

00:15:56.450 --> 00:16:00.470
move on. You know, you don't need to, uh, defeat every enemy.

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:06.990
You don't need to, um, like the keys aren't actually locked behind anything.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:07.810
They're just hidden.

00:16:07.910 --> 00:16:09.510
You just have to find them. Yeah, you have to find them.

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.650
I can't remember why I can't remember why I can't remember why I can't remember why

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:39.490
why I can't remember why I can't remember why I can't remember why I can't remember why I can't remember why I can't remember why I can remember why I can't remember why I can remember why I can remember

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:45.410
Before we move any further, though, we do need to talk about the controls of the game

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:49.330
because I think this is where the game falls apart immediately.

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:53.650
Sean, do you want to walk us through your gameplay situation?

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:57.490
Yeah. I don't know if it's so much the control scheme itself.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:16:59.210
The control scheme is pretty basic.

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:03.010
It's, you know, B is attack or A is, I don't know.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.470
I think B is attack, A is jump.

00:17:04.590 --> 00:17:05.910
Yeah, B is attack, A is jump.

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:10.450
Your jump is very Mario in which, you know, you can control in midair.

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:12.250
It's got enough floatiness to it.

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:13.910
So it's not the base controls.

00:17:14.110 --> 00:17:20.830
It's more the animation and, like, the actual act of attacking

00:17:20.830 --> 00:17:28.330
because I think that this game is the closest it's ever gotten to the player character

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:31.330
wielding a sword in the same way that I would

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:34.110
because it doesn't so much, like, attack or slash.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.290
It's more just sort of wiggles it around in front of him.

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:37.850
He just, like, lets things bump into the sword.

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:45.030
I've never played a game with such, like, a fruitless attack animation.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:49.170
But I feel like that only matters until you get the knife,

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:50.730
and then I never use the sword again.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:55.590
Well, sure. I mean, like, you know, you get the little projectile with it,

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:58.570
but it just feels so toothless.

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:02.110
It's funny, though. I never feel like I'm actually a threat in this game.

00:18:02.110 --> 00:18:04.130
No, I think you're definitely right because I actually,

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:05.350
until you guys are talking about this,

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:07.370
kind of forgot how the sword controlled at all

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:10.910
because I was never inclined to use it again once I got that throwing knife.

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:12.050
Like, that was my primary means of attack.

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:16.490
And not only that, your jump does also count as an attack as well.

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:18.850
So I was mostly using that, if we're being honest,

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:20.830
just attacking things from above me

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:26.030
because while this game is both vertical and horizontal in exploration,

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:28.270
it's a lot more vertically focused.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:32.090
It leans more. So I was just using that to attack things above me.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.470
Or if you're skilled enough with it,

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:38.290
you can hurt almost anything while you're jumping with the sword.

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:41.430
And it's weird because he's, like, he's doing, like, a pencil dive,

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:43.910
but he has his knife out, like, diagonal,

00:18:43.930 --> 00:18:46.230
so it winds up taking a lot of things out.

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:51.430
But the actual sword fight enemy animation thing of you

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:53.710
is exactly how Sean is describing it.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:56.130
It's this small little, like, wiggle of a sword.

00:18:56.290 --> 00:19:00.230
And I feel like the hitbox range is realistic to what the guy is doing,

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:03.550
but I can't help but feel like if he would just lunge his sword out a little,

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:06.110
more things would be taken care of.

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:10.310
It was the opposite of a power trip, the way that this character controls.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:14.850
Right. And the levels themselves, exploration-wise,

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:16.650
how did you feel about that?

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:20.730
So I felt like design of the environment was cool.

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:22.190
That was the first thing that stood out to me.

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:28.870
But design of the level itself felt not particularly deliberate,

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:31.090
like we see in a lot of other games.

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:32.390
And I think that's –

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:35.850
I know it wasn't the worst offender of this by a long shot,

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:37.970
but I think a lot of times we have a problem when there's, like,

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:41.350
enemies kind of appear at what seems like a random time,

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:44.830
and especially when you're in that, like, forest level in the beginning.

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:46.430
There's just enemies everywhere.

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:48.090
And it's a little much.

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:50.110
What happened at that forest level?

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:51.310
Can we talk about that for a minute?

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:55.190
Because there's all these stumps, like, all over the place, right,

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:59.510
that look like they're in varying ranges of the third dimension.

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:01.630
You know, like some look further away from you.

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:03.570
Some look like they're right next to each other.

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:07.410
But as long as it's got a platform on that stump, you can land on it.

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:09.950
So is my character also jumping, like –

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:12.270
Backwards? Yeah, backwards to land on some of these?

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:13.970
It's got a very strange perspective.

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:19.070
And, like, in a more general sense, though,

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:22.290
I think that the reliance on a more –

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:26.250
at least in this level, because I didn't play too many more –

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:32.110
the reliance on the verticality I think hindered this game

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:34.950
because there are only –

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:39.030
I don't know how many screens wide this level is,

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:42.970
but most of what you have to do is just jumping and jumping,

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:44.530
and if you miss, you fall back down.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:49.230
And it's just an act of frustration navigating this level,

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:52.590
especially when you might just jump through a door you didn't mean to.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:54.470
You can continue.

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:58.470
I'm sorry. No, just a clarification of that is that they don't act like screens.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:00.190
It actually is free-flowing.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:01.090
You can –

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:02.230
Yes. Like, you know, it's not –

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.190
it's not like when you get to the end of the screen –

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:07.390
Yeah, it scrolls. It just does that seamlessly.

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:10.470
But to completely agree with Sean's point,

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:14.390
the verticality is only weird because the jumping in this game –

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:16.870
I was kind of surprised to hear it be referred to like Mario.

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:18.690
Yes, it's Mario in the sense that, like,

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:21.990
you can control while you're in the air, which is nice.

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:27.590
Yeah. But the actual, like, landing areas for a lot of the things in this game,

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:32.250
regardless of if it's like a tree branch or a little piece of a rock inside of a cave,

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:35.590
a lot of those things are very small and easily missed,

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:38.630
and then you do fall back to the beginning of the level.

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:40.110
This being –

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:44.530
It seems like a – I don't know what the rule is for that.

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:47.050
I feel like sometimes when I was falling –

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:48.950
Sometimes you won't. Yeah, I could hit a platform and land and stop myself,

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.330
but other times I'd just fall straight through.

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:51.810
I think there's a certain –

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:56.330
There's actually a certain amount that you can fall before you just start free-falling.

00:21:56.330 --> 00:21:59.510
I know what you're talking about. And the free-falling, seemingly, then you fall back to the beginning.

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:02.630
But sometimes in free-fall, I was interrupted by a platform that saved me.

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:04.730
Oh, okay. So I don't know what triggers that.

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:08.150
The rules of movement in this game just seem very haphazard.

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:12.130
Yes. And I think the verticality thing would have been interesting

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:19.570
if the things that you needed to collect didn't go away once you had collected them

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:20.770
because then that would be like,

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:23.810
Oh, okay, well, I'm back down here, but at least I can find new things to collect.

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:27.210
Once you've gotten every single gem at the bottom,

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:28.630
when you fall back down to the bottom,

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:30.970
now it's like, okay, cool, I just got to go climb this whole thing again.

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:34.370
Well, I think that regeneration of those things could just –

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:36.090
cause you to break the game.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:39.010
Absolutely. I'm actually not saying that it's a valid point.

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:41.630
I'm just saying that it kind of stinks when you fall and there's nothing.

00:22:41.870 --> 00:22:44.650
Now it's just literally the only thing to do is to climb back up to the top.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:47.010
Yeah, and I will say, I think you're kind of saying this,

00:22:47.050 --> 00:22:48.170
but I just want to articulate that.

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:51.810
I think that the jump itself feels good.

00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:54.650
I think that the problem is that the platforms are poorly designed.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:56.250
Yes, absolutely. I think the jump –

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:57.770
I think maybe that's why people say it's like Mario.

00:22:58.030 --> 00:23:00.770
I know that when Mario lands, he takes like a few steps,

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:02.530
and we've talked about how that gives him weight.

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:04.330
Yeah. And I feel that in this game.

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:07.190
But if you have that weight and you move a few steps and you land,

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:10.830
and there's one pixel to land on, that makes it very difficult.

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:14.830
Yeah. Right. And this is most annoying just to continue on that first level

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:16.510
when you're climbing through the trees and stuff.

00:23:16.870 --> 00:23:19.590
There's – besides all the weird stumps all around,

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:23.090
there's also these perfect doors inside the trees that like –

00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:24.430
who knows how those got there.

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:29.370
But you go inside the trees then, and those are entirely vertical levels.

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:34.210
Yeah. Where they're all filled with just little stumps that like –

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:35.970
it kind of reminds me of Metroid.

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:40.770
Yeah. When you're in like a very vertical area, and it's like if you fell the wrong way,

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:44.530
you'd have to climb your way back through there, which is fine in Metroid.

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:47.530
For some reason, it's not fine here because in Metroid,

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.470
the whole idea is like you're in this cave and stuff like that.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:53.930
For this, it's like I'm really just climbing a tree.

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:57.350
You feel more of a sense of space in Metroid in the sense that you like –

00:23:57.350 --> 00:24:00.230
you know where you are and you've gotten familiar with the map and you like the map.

00:24:00.230 --> 00:24:04.230
It's actually like a handmade design of like enemy placement.

00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:10.250
Right. Whereas in this, like if you can – you can't catch your breath because if you fall,

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:14.590
you'll get attacked by some bees or whatever, and then you get rid of the bees

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:16.570
and you're like, okay, now I can finally start making my way up.

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:17.870
And more bees come.

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:21.490
Right. And if it's not bees, it's spiders.

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:24.390
Bees are in that final tree of this level.

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:25.450
I know what you're talking about.

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:29.990
There's randomly just like all of a sudden bees showing up before the first boss,

00:24:29.990 --> 00:24:31.670
which is just a giant floating skull.

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:33.890
We'll get to all that in a minute.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:37.650
So the other thing to mention about exploration, though,

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:43.950
is that this might be the least punishing game of all time in the other respect

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:47.250
where it's like I just wish that if I would have fell from a blinding height

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:51.210
that it would just kill me because then at least I would respawn immediately

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:55.490
where I started because that's exactly what happens whenever you die.

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:59.130
You respawn exactly where you should be.

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:00.970
Where you died. Yeah, where you died, right?

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:02.790
And you have unlimited continues.

00:25:03.070 --> 00:25:05.630
So now I'm on the fence about that lack of punishment

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:08.350
because usually I like to be like a good middle ground.

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:11.870
But I think that because this game has that like jankiness,

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:13.650
I was trying to figure this out earlier.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:17.090
I was like what I think that made for me the game a lot more palatable

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:21.130
because then I was like, okay, if I did have to do what I do in most games

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:23.830
and go back to the beginning of the level or back even to a checkpoint,

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:25.810
I would probably have shut the game off.

00:25:25.810 --> 00:25:29.810
But I will say, reading the room and thinking that no one's going to agree with me,

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:32.110
I had fun playing this game.

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:36.550
I really did. And I 100% agree with all the bad things we're saying about it.

00:25:36.610 --> 00:25:38.010
I don't want to spoil anything.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:41.470
I don't want to color my view so fast.

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:43.830
I'm not saying whether I had a good or bad time.

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:47.770
I'm more curious though because we've been talking about this continues thing

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:50.850
since like the first launch games for the NES.

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:54.730
We finally have seen a game that practices unlimited continues

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:56.470
with almost no repercussion.

00:25:56.750 --> 00:25:57.790
How do we feel about that?

00:25:57.950 --> 00:25:59.310
Now that we're seeing it in practice,

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:01.850
would you have wished that other games would have handled it this way?

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:07.970
I think it just feels a little out of place in terms of like where we are

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:12.710
in this retrospective because I'm so used to it with games nowadays.

00:26:12.710 --> 00:26:15.390
Like I know in The Last of Us, as soon as you die,

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:19.050
you get respawned like 15 feet behind you and you just keep going.

00:26:19.270 --> 00:26:20.650
So I'm used to that.

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:25.570
It's just odd to do it in a game that looks and feels like this.

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:27.150
Right. I do think that, like I said,

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:31.050
it made this game because of the flaws in the game more palatable to play.

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:34.910
In general, I think that there's, like I said, a middle ground.

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:38.110
Even from The Last of Us, I think as much as I love that game,

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:42.310
I think that could benefit from a little more punishment,

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:43.830
just something to make the stakes higher.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:49.610
Yeah, because I mean you even keep the items you've collected when you die.

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:52.430
And so I think I'm agreeing with Joe on this,

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:53.970
and not that you didn't say this, Sean.

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:55.590
I just think like...

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:56.390
He just likes me better.

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:59.950
Just to follow up with the point is that I think the middle room is that

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:01.170
all games, in my opinion,

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:03.650
and I understand why they didn't do this back then.

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:05.730
We're looking at it 30 years later.

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:08.950
All of these games should have unlimited continues.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:11.310
I think seeing unlimited continues in this game is like,

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:14.110
yes, thank goodness I don't have to collect all that stuff again.

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:15.210
Like I already did it.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:17.170
I got the satisfaction of collecting it.

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:21.450
The satisfaction of me beating games has nothing to do with how hard they are.

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:23.090
I don't get satisfied by that.

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:25.610
I get satisfied by checking off the tasks.

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:28.670
In this game, you collect the gems, then you collect the keys,

00:27:28.950 --> 00:27:31.130
then you beat the boss, and then you move on to the next level.

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:34.310
Once I collect the gems, I don't want to do it again because I died.

00:27:34.370 --> 00:27:35.350
That's true. You know?

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:40.190
So I think that the only alternative answer is like,

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:43.590
when you die, you also, because there's no live system,

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:45.930
it's more like a Super Mario Odyssey system,

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:48.370
where you also lose like 20 gems,

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:50.930
and they go back to like a different place or something.

00:27:50.990 --> 00:27:51.890
Maybe 20 is too much.

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:55.230
Maybe it's just five. But like that's a creative way.

00:27:55.370 --> 00:27:56.770
But you know, that's a lot of programming to do.

00:27:56.850 --> 00:27:57.910
So I'm not saying it was possible.

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:00.710
I'm saying that's my 30-year-later solve.

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:03.610
Yeah. Like when I think of Ghosts and Goblins,

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:08.030
granted that didn't have as much punishment as a lot of games on the NES

00:28:08.030 --> 00:28:09.330
as far as like how far it sends you back.

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:12.670
But I didn't mind.

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:15.650
I mean, you know, it would get frustrating in like a good way

00:28:15.650 --> 00:28:18.390
when I died over and over again and had to keep doing the same part.

00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:19.830
But that's like you're learning that part,

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:21.550
and you're like, okay, this is the part with the Red Devils,

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:23.930
and this is this part. Obviously, there were flaws in that too.

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:29.490
But the idea being that like I was okay with generally with re-traversing those areas

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:30.350
because they were still fun.

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:32.650
In this game, if I had to go back and re-traverse some of these areas,

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:35.390
I'd be like, oh, yeah, this whole bland area again.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:37.070
And then I would get much more tired of it.

00:28:37.150 --> 00:28:39.270
Sure, sure. Ghosts and Goblins, though, just to clarify,

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:44.290
I think is still in line with what I'm thinking is a good recipe for this kind of game

00:28:44.290 --> 00:28:46.910
because that did have a couple of checkpoints too.

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:50.850
And also like unlimited continues in the sense that like it would take you back to the screen,

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:55.730
but you could start right where you were on the level that you were last on.

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:56.810
So that was still good.

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:58.970
And that's like a good punishment because it didn't like –

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:00.670
it wasn't like, oh, that Red Devil killed you?

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:02.390
Well, now you spawn right in front of that Red Devil again.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:03.950
Exactly. It's a good in-between.

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:07.270
I'm also never an advocate of start the whole game over when you die.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:14.910
I think that's what I'm saying is like the worst thing that we continually see on most of these NES games

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:17.610
is like I know that that's like a –

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:19.850
This is a little off topic.

00:29:20.070 --> 00:29:20.930
No, I don't think it's okay.

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:22.290
No, no, no, I don't mean that. What are your thoughts?

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:23.150
What I'm about to say.

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:29.690
I'd love to hear it. But what are your thoughts then on like the ever-expanding genre of roguelike,

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:32.750
roguelite, like those kinds of games?

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:37.370
What I like about those games, and you can clarify for me,

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:40.050
but for me, from the ones that I've played,

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:45.190
is that with that, every single time you play, it's mostly a new experience.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:48.810
There might be new items that you find as you're traversing through.

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:50.710
The map layout might be different.

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:52.590
The enemies you encounter might be different.

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:57.270
So that's not as punishing because at least I'm getting an entirely different experience.

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:01.550
When you're playing through Karate Kid and you die and you have to start back at the beginning,

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:03.930
you're just doing the same fights again and again.

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:07.270
When you say roguelike, are you talking about procedurally generated things?

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:08.590
There usually are. Okay. Yeah.

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:10.270
Like Binding of Isaac.

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:15.770
Binding of Isaac. Okay. The one I just played, Streets of Rogue.

00:30:15.970 --> 00:30:16.890
Streets of Rogue. Okay.

00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:22.470
But they're, yeah, it's basically like a new experience, a very similar experience.

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:29.390
They also usually have like an overarching progression where you can unlock stuff after you die.

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:30.230
Right. Or, yeah.

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:31.710
And I love that part, too.

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:35.670
I think that's a really interesting concept that I would love to see in some NES game.

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:36.850
I don't know if we're ever going to see it.

00:30:37.030 --> 00:30:48.230
But it's this idea that like as you get like more experience playing the game, you unlock things that then can be found somewhere in the world of that game if you get like a lucky roll.

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:55.130
Like I think that would be really cool to see if a Legend of Zelda 2 proper to the Legend of Zelda 1 ever came out.

00:30:55.410 --> 00:31:01.770
Like something like that where it's like, hey, now in your treasure chest in Dungeon 1, you might find this instead of that.

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:03.470
Like that would have been really cool.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:05.290
Because that's kind of what Binding of Isaac is, right?

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:06.270
Yeah. Pretty much.

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:07.810
Anyway. Yeah.

00:31:08.130 --> 00:31:09.310
Tangent. No, that's a tangent.

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:11.050
I think we're done with the continues, too, right?

00:31:11.130 --> 00:31:12.390
Yes. Nobody else has any thoughts on that?

